\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Energy sign and the slice kernel}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{For a \vocabk{normal transfer}{fdip-01BF} #{T} on a
single slice, let #{H'=E_sK'} be its
\vocabk{graded slice kernel}{fdip-01BF}. If #{B_+>0},
##{\nu_\pm(\bbI -T)=\nu_\pm(H'),\qquad
\ker(\bbI -T)=\ker H'.}
In particular these are the energy-sign dimensions when
#{T>0}. The positive subspaces can be distinct.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{t=a_s}, let #{\iota:W_+\to W} be inclusion and
put #{Q=t^{-1}B_+}, #{X=(\bbI -T)/t}. On the orthogonal
ranges of #{P_+,P_-} the restrictions of #{H'} have the form
##{H'=\begin{pmatrix}
 A_+ & E\\ E^\dagger & -A_-
\end{pmatrix}.}
From the defining equations for #{T},
##{X=H'-H'\iota Q^{-1}\iota^\dagger H'.}
Indeed its upper restrictions are #{A_+B_+^{-1}} and
#{B_+^{-1}E}, since #{B_+=\bbI_++tA_+}. The lower ones
are #{E^\dagger B_+^{-1}} and #{-A_--tE^\dagger B_+^{-1}E},
which are the same terms obtained by subtraction on the right.}

\p{On #{W\oplus W_+} the form
##{\langle z+\iota w,H'(z+\iota w)\rangle+t^{-1}\|w\|^2}
is congruent to the form of #{H'\oplus t^{-1}\bbI_+} by
the invertible shear #{(z,w)\mapsto(z+\iota w,w)}.
Completing the square in #{w} gives
##{\langle z,Xz\rangle+
\langle w+Q^{-1}\iota^\dagger H'z,\,
Q(w+Q^{-1}\iota^\dagger H'z)\rangle .}
The second expression is congruent to #{X\oplus Q}.
An invertible change of variables preserves the maximal dimensions
of strictly positive and strictly negative subspaces. For either
sign this maximum is the dimension of its spectral subspace:
orthogonal projection onto that subspace is injective on every
definite subspace of that sign. As #{Q} and #{t^{-1}\bbI_+}
are positive on the same space, their contributions cancel.
Thus #{X} and #{H'} have equal sign dimensions. The identity
for #{X} gives #{\ker H'\subseteq\ker X}; equal nullities
give equality of kernels, and #{t>0} gives the formula.}

\p{For distinct sign spaces use the second slice of
\ref{fdip-01BJ} with spin dimension #{2}. There
#{A=\bbI}, #{B_+=2\bbI_+}, and #{E:W_-\to W_+} is
unitary. Hence #{(H')^2=2\bbI}, #{\trace H'=0}, and
#{\bbI -T=H'/2-P_-}. This operator does not commute with
#{H'} because #{E\ne0}. If their positive lines coincided,
so would their orthogonal negative lines, and the two
operators would commute.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.fromBlocks_eq_of_invertible₂₂} gives
the factorization underlying completion of the square at an
invertible lower-right restriction.}
